{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1653 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e3ddba6bbd3e54b8d4ce5c1b9d529708435d904fbdffe7f288cd5005aec9e97d" :edb/db-before-t 1652 :edb/db-after-t 1653 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534965 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:22:56.450Z" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124167 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n$f_x(x,y) = 6xy+axy^3-5, \\qquad f_y(x,y) = 3x^2+bx^2y^2, \\qquad f_y(2,-1) = -24$." 13194139534965 false] [17592186124167 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b,$ if \n\n$$\nf_x(x,y) = 6xy+axy^3-5, \\qquad\nf_y(x,y) = 3x^2+bx^2y^2, \\qquad\nf_y(2,-1) = -24.\n$$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124167 1145 "First, we compute the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{xy}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(f_{x}(x, y)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy + axy^{3} - 5) \\\\\n&= 6x + 3axy^{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= 6x + 3axy^{2} \\\\\nf_{yx}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(f_{y}(x, y)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} + bx^{2}y^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6x + 2bxy^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. So, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{xy}(x, y) &= f_{yx}(x, y) \\\\\n6x + 3axy^{2} &= 6x + 2bxy^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy^2$, we see that $3a=2b$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf_y(2,-1)=-24\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{y}(2,-1) &= -24 \\\\\n3(2)^{2} + b(2)^{2}(-1)^{2} &= -24 \\\\\n12 + 4b &= -24 \\\\\n4b &= -36 \\\\\nb &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= 2b \\\\\n3a &= 2(-9) \\\\\n3a &= -18 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-6$ and $b=-9$." 13194139534965 false] [17592186124167 1145 "First, we compute the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+ax{y}^{3}-5) \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+b{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. So, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yx}(x,y) \\\\ 6x+3ax{y}^{2} & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy^2,$ we see that $3a=2b.$ \n\nNow, since $f_y(2,-1)=-24,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-24 \\\\ 3(2)^{2}+b(2)^{2}(-1)^{2} & =-24 \\\\ 12+4b & =-24 \\\\ 4b & =-36 \\\\ b & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & =2b \\\\ 3a & =2(-9) \\\\ 3a & =-18 \\\\ a & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-6$ and $b=-9.$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124168 1100 "Consider the function $f(x,y,z)$. Given that all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, and that\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z\\partial x} = -e^xy\\sin{(yz)}$,\n\nevaluate the following expression at the point $(1,\\pi,1)$:\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial z\\partial x\\partial z}+ \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial x \\partial z \\partial y}$" 13194139534965 false] [17592186124168 1100 "Consider the function $f(x,y,z).$ Given that all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z\\partial x} = -e^xy\\sin{(yz)},\n$$\n\n \n\nevaluate the following expression at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial z\\partial x\\partial z}+ \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f }{\\partial x \\partial z \\partial y}\n$$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124168 1145 "Since the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} &= \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂z∂x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(-e^{x}y\\sin (yz)) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}y^{2}\\cos (yz)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-e^{x}y\\sin (yz)) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\sin (yz) - e^{x}yz\\cos (yz) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}(\\sin (yz) + yz\\cos (yz))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,\\pi,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & _{(1, π, 1)} &= -e^{(1)}(π)^{2}\\cos (π \\cdot 1) = eπ^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} & _{(1, π, 1)}|=-e^{(1)}(\\sin (π \\cdot 1) + (π)(1)\\cos (π \\cdot 1)) = eπ\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} \\mid _{(1, π, 1)} + \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} \\mid _{(1, π, 1)} &= eπ^{2} + eπ = eπ(π + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534965 false] [17592186124168 1145 "Since the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂z∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)-{e}^{x}yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)+yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\pi )^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=e{\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)+(\\pi )(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1))=e\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =e{\\pi }^{2}+e\\pi =e\\pi (\\pi +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124169 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$?\n\n1. $f_x(x,y)=x+2y$ and $f_y(x,y)=2y-x$\n2. $f_x(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$ and $f_y(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$\n3. $f_x(x,y)=-\\sin{x}\\sin{y}$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos{x}\\cos{y}$" 13194139534965 false] [17592186124169 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=x+2y$ and $f_y(x,y)=2y-x$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$ and $f_y(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=-\\sin{x}\\sin{y}$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos{x}\\cos{y}$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124169 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is,\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y)\n$$\n\nLet's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + 2y) = 2,; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y - x) =-1$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) \\neq f_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could *not* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n- For the second pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5(x + y)^{4}) = 20(x + y)^{3},; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5(x + y)^{4}) = 20(x + y)^{3}$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) = f_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n- For the third pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sin x\\sin y) =-\\sin x\\cos y,; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\cos x\\cos y) =-\\sin x\\cos y$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534965 false] [17592186124169 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is,\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y).\n$$\n\nLet's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) \\neq f_{yx}(x,y),$ the first pair could *not* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) = f_{yx}(x,y),$ the second pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y),$  the third pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323136 13194139534965 true] [17592186124366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323192 13194139534965 true] [17592186124366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323199 13194139534965 true] [17592186124366 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323213 13194139534965 true] [17592186124367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323157 13194139534965 true] [17592186124367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323164 13194139534965 true] [17592186124367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323171 13194139534965 true] [17592186124367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323178 13194139534965 true] [17592186124367 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323185 13194139534965 true] [17592186124368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323143 13194139534965 true] [17592186124368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323150 13194139534965 true] [17592186124368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323206 13194139534965 true] [17592186124914 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534965 true] [17592186124914 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. $f_{x}(x, y) = x + 4y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = 3x - y$\n2. $f_{x}(x, y) = 2x$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = 0$\n3. $f_{x}(x, y) = 2x + \\cos x\\cos y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = e^{y} - \\sin x\\sin y$" 13194139534965 false] [17592186124914 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=0$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186124914 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+4y)=4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139534965 true] [17592186125102 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534965 true] [17592186125102 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. $f_{x}(x, y) = x^{3}y - x$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = xy^{3} - y$\n2. $f_{x}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2x$\n3. $f_{x}(x, y) = \\sqrt{x - 1}$ and $f_{y}(x, y) =-3$" 13194139534965 false] [17592186125102 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{3}y-x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186125102 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x)={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y)={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}-2y)={e}^{x+y}-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}-2x)={e}^{x+y}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x-1})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139534965 true] [17592186125103 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534965 true] [17592186125103 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n\n$f_{x}(x, y) = ax^{2}y - 4y^{2} + 3, f_{y}(x, y) = 2x^{3} + bxy - 8$." 13194139534965 false] [17592186125103 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}+bxy-8.\n$$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186125103 1145 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}-8y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+bxy-8) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}-8y=6{x}^{2}+by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=6$ and $b=-8.$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323136 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323137 13194139534965 true] [17592186323136 1089 "q-133221" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323136 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534965 true] [17592186323136 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=axy-5{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-bxy.\n$$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323136 1113 #uuid "c7b780a7-788b-5d60-b089-16f56470436d" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323136 1145 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(axy-5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =ax-10y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-bxy) \\\\  & =2x-by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-10y=2x-by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=2$ and $b=10.$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323138 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323139 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323140 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323141 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323142 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1041 "selection" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323140 13194139534965 true] [17592186323137 1110 #uuid "2ca200be-837d-52f4-bd44-1d4f38f751d3" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323138 1034 "a=5,\\,b=2" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534965 true] [17592186323138 1111 #uuid "d1715457-c5bd-515f-93ed-a4940bbc67a6" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323139 1034 "a=3,\\,b=7" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534965 true] [17592186323139 1111 #uuid "2abbda83-d1b3-5f14-9940-7bfc4b624ec0" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323140 1034 "a=2,\\,b=10" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323140 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534965 true] [17592186323140 1111 #uuid "cbf1cf60-e038-5d2d-9dd1-03b55f43a97c" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323141 1034 "a=6,\\,b=9" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534965 true] [17592186323141 1111 #uuid "4c4f9f91-2059-51ad-b83c-1692b7fd76c2" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323142 1034 "a=2,\\,b=5" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534965 true] [17592186323142 1111 #uuid "da47f82d-bfe4-5b61-980b-5e300f5fc698" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323143 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323144 13194139534965 true] [17592186323143 1089 "q-133250" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323143 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534965 true] [17592186323143 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323143 1113 #uuid "3ec7f385-eb59-5b15-b685-39bfa2ef206b" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323143 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323144 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323145 13194139534965 true] [17592186323144