derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4) \\\\  & =a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1) \\\\  & =(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y}=(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x},$ respectively, we see that $a=-b.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{x}(1,1)=4+5e$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e \\\\ a(1)(1+(1))e+(1)e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ 2ae+e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ (2a+1)e & =5e \\\\ 2a+1 & =5 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb=-a=-2.\n$$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323201 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323202 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323203 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323204 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323205 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1041 "selection" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323202 13194139534965 true] [17592186323200 1110 #uuid "d1f6ac57-51cf-509d-8565-1e772997a007" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323201 1034 "$a=2$, $b=-4$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323201 1111 #uuid "2338eab1-7879-5aa0-844a-f9ae5ca48f4c" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323202 1034 "$a=2$, $b=-2$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323202 1111 #uuid "54d0e5c2-a740-5c7c-ab84-44ac40e4ba8d" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323203 1034 "$a=2$, $b=1$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323203 1111 #uuid "5fa21fed-af6a-5271-b73d-c7110db252e2" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323204 1034 "$a=4$, $b=2$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323204 1111 #uuid "6357b2bc-815d-5836-a230-eea14bd1ea47" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323205 1034 "$a=1$, $b=-2$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323205 1111 #uuid "b7a50334-a5e2-55f8-8d6c-d6d3eafd71db" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323206 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323207 13194139534965 true] [17592186323206 1089 "q-80753" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534965 true] [17592186323206 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323206 1113 #uuid "7ba4f358-7a33-51dd-acb9-48ca0f438659" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323206 1145 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323208 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323209 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323210 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323211 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323212 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1041 "selection" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323208 13194139534965 true] [17592186323207 1110 #uuid "2f6d6e27-c0b4-5426-a830-c42d01a5f2e5" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323208 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323208 1111 #uuid "3693dd69-b57f-5c1f-bb68-959816b69ab2" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323209 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323209 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323209 1111 #uuid "956f378e-ea70-5473-b6dd-1908244a5228" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323210 1034 "III only" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323210 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323210 1111 #uuid "6a18faa3-dff2-5941-ba6d-4c6141863c00" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323211 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323211 1111 #uuid "c36a3595-debd-59cf-8ba9-7cceb685bef7" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323212 1034 "II only" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534965 true] [17592186323212 1111 #uuid "56e6d81f-21ef-5199-a42c-16f700341789" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323213 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323214 13194139534965 true] [17592186323213 1089 "q-80766" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323213 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534965 true] [17592186323213 1100 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323213 1113 #uuid "f63374b4-82d5-5092-bcef-8ec87e149ab1" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323213 1145 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}=b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x}$ and $x{y}^{2},$ respectively, we see that $b=1$ and $a=-2.$" 13194139534965 true] [17592186323214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323215 13194139534965 true] [17592186323214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323216 13194139534965 true] [17592186323214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323217 13194139534965 true] [17592186323214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323218 13194139534965 true] [17592186323214 1000 #edb/ref 1759